bodyText:
For a rental of a dwelling unit, Florida's Residential Landlord and Tenant Act requires these notices in writing:
- (1) the tenant's 7-day notice to end the lease for your material noncompliance, your 7-day notices for the tenant's noncompliance, and your 3-day notice demanding rent or possession (Fla. Stat. § 83.56(1)-(3)); these must be mailed, hand-delivered, e-mailed under a signed e-mail addendum, or left at the home if the tenant is absent, and the lease cannot waive those requirements (Fla. Stat. § 83.56(4));
- (2) either party's notice ending a week-to-week, month-to-month, quarter-to-quarter or year-to-year tenancy, delivered the same way (Fla. Stat. § 83.57);
- (3) your reminder notice before an end-of-term notice period, if the lease uses that option (Fla. Stat. § 83.575(2));
- (4) your notice of a claim on the security deposit, by certified mail or agreed e-mail, within 30 days after the lease ends (Fla. Stat. § 83.49(3)(a)), and the deposit-holding notice if you rent five or more units (Fla. Stat. § 83.49(2));
- (5) the 7-day notice, by certified mail or personal delivery, that a tenant must give before vacating early (before the lease term ends) or leaving a week-to-week, month-to-month, quarter-to-quarter or year-to-year tenancy, unless a written lease provides otherwise (Fla. Stat. § 83.49(5));
- (6) your name and address for notices, at or before the tenancy starts (Fla. Stat. § 83.50);
- and (7) a servicemember's notice ending the lease (Fla. Stat. § 83.682(1)).

Your notice before entering to make repairs need only be reasonable notice, which the statute defines as at least 24 hours before entry; the statute does not require it to be written (Fla. Stat. § 83.53(2)). Putting it in writing is still the safer practice.

Florida's Act sets seven duties that every tenant of a dwelling unit owes at all times during the tenancy, whatever the lease says:
- (1) comply with the obligations building, housing and health codes place on tenants;
- (2) keep the part of the premises the tenant occupies and uses clean and sanitary;
- (3) remove all garbage from the unit in a clean and sanitary manner;
- (4) keep all plumbing fixtures in the unit, or used by the tenant, clean, sanitary and in repair;
- (5) use and operate electrical, plumbing, sanitary, heating, ventilating and air-conditioning facilities and appliances, including elevators, in a reasonable manner;
- (6) not destroy, deface, damage, impair or remove any part of the premises or your property in it, nor let anyone else do so;
- and (7) behave, and require anyone on the premises with the tenant's consent to behave, so as not to unreasonably disturb the neighbors or breach the peace.

The tenant must also not unreasonably withhold consent to your entry for the purposes the Act lists.

Because these duties are in the statute, the lease does not need to repeat them; the library's tenant-maintenance and no-disturbance clauses add detail on top of them.

If the tenant materially fails to comply with these duties, the lease or reasonable rules, you may give a 7-day notice: a notice to cure for breaches the tenant should be allowed to fix, such as failing to keep the premises clean and sanitary, or a notice of termination without a chance to cure for breaches such as intentional destruction, damage or misuse of your or other tenants' property or a repeated unreasonable disturbance (see "Eviction Notices and Process"). You may also recover the damages the breach causes (see "When the Tenant Causes Damage").
